# AQP Management Engine — credential safety (always on)

This rule is the transcript-boundary enforcement of the
`aqp-management-engine` subagent contract. It applies to every
file in this repository because the same agent surface is reached
from many touch points (subagent invocations, ad-hoc tool calls,
smoke scripts, manual debugging).

## Never print / log / echo

The following values MUST NEVER appear in:

- chat / agent transcripts (visible to the user OR a reviewer)
- structured log lines (`logger.info`, `logger.warning`, …)
- `print(...)` output from any module under `aqp/`, `scripts/`,
  `aqp_control_plane/`, `aqp_platform_core/`, or `frontend/src/`
- `console.log` / `console.warn` output from `frontend/src/` or
  any file under `theia-extensions/aqp/`
- audit ledger rows (`workload_runs`, `security_audit_events`,
  `terraform_runs`, `agent_runs_v2`, `assistant_runs`)
- exception messages re-raised back to the route layer
- response bodies returned from any `/auth/*`, `/manage/*`,
  `/cluster/*`, `/cloudflare/*`, `/terraform/*`, or `/tenancy/*`
  endpoint

Forbidden values:

- Access tokens, refresh tokens, id tokens (full or truncated
  beyond the safe 4-character debug prefix described below)
- M2M client_secrets, OAuth client_secrets, Auth0 Management API
  tokens
- MFA enrollment secrets, recovery codes, TOTP seeds
- Raw tenancy invite tokens (only the HMAC hash + `token_prefix`
  may be persisted; the raw token returns once on create)
- `secondary_jwt` payloads from the link-account flow
- Cloudflare API tokens (`CLOUDFLARE_API_TOKEN`), tunnel secrets
  (`tunnel_secret`), Access app `client_secret` values
- `Cf-Access-Jwt-Assertion` header values (the JWT itself, not
  just the header name)
- Kubernetes kubeconfig contents, ServiceAccount tokens, Strimzi
  user secrets, KafkaUser passwords
- Cloud-provider credentials: `~/.aws/credentials`,
  `~/.azure/credentials`, `~/.config/gcloud/`, Workload Identity
  Federation JWTs, federated tokens
- `cloudflared/cert.pem` / tunnel credentials files
- Full `Authorization` headers or any dump of `request.headers`
  (including `httpx` request reprs, `curl -v` output, Theia
  WebSocket frame dumps)

## Allowed: opt-in redacted prefixes

For local debugging ONLY, code MAY print the first 4 characters
of a token (no more) followed by an ellipsis, but ONLY behind an
explicit operator opt-in flag:

```python
# OK — guarded by an explicit unsafe-print flag
if args.unsafe_print_token_prefixes:
    print(f"  access_token: {tokens.access_token[:4]}…")
else:
    print("  access_token: <redacted>")
```

The flag MUST default to off, MUST live in a `scripts/` smoke
test or a developer-only utility (never in `aqp/` route or task
code), and MUST NOT be enabled in CI.

## Never load + print

Code MUST NOT read these files and emit their contents:

- `.env`, `.env.local`, `.env.production`
- `~/.kube/config`, `~/.config/aqp/credentials/*.json`
- `~/.aws/credentials`, `~/.aws/config`
- `~/.azure/credentials`, `~/.azure/auth`
- `~/.config/gcloud/application_default_credentials.json`,
  `~/.config/gcloud/legacy_credentials/`
- `cloudflared/cert.pem`, `cloudflared/<tunnel-id>.json`

When debugging credential resolution, use the existing
`CredentialResolver.describe()` surface — it returns store kind
+ priority + alias only, never the resolved fields.

## Forbidden code patterns

The following patterns are direct rule violations:

```python
# BAD
print(f"token: {access_token[:18]}...")
logger.info("calling %s with header %s", url, request.headers)
logger.debug("auth bearer %s", authorization)
return {"token": tokens.access_token}  # in an audit endpoint
```

```typescript
// BAD
console.warn("getAccessTokenSilently failed:", err);  // err may embed the token
console.log("Bearer", token);
toast.info(`refreshed: ${result.accessToken}`);
```

## Required code patterns

```python
# OK
print(f"  access_token: {_safe_token(tokens.access_token)}")
logger.warning("getAccessTokenSilently failed: code=%s", code or "unknown")
```

```typescript
// OK
const code = (err as { error?: string })?.error;
console.warn("getAccessTokenSilently failed: code=%s", code ?? "unknown");
```

## Audit ledger redaction

The shared `WorkloadRuntime` redaction in
[aqp_platform_core/runtime/workload.py](../../aqp_platform_core/src/aqp_platform_core/runtime/workload.py)
strips any key whose lowercased name contains `password`,
`secret`, `token`, `key`, `credential`, `private`,
`authorization`, `kubeconfig`, `client_secret`, `api_token`,
`api_key`, `jwt`, `refresh_token`, or `access_token`. New
audit-writing code MUST go through this helper — do not write
your own.

## When the subagent operates

The `aqp-management-engine` subagent
(`.cursor/agents/aqp-management-engine.md`) reads this rule on
every invocation. The subagent will refuse to execute any
sub-step that would surface a forbidden value to the user, and
will recommend the safe alternative (e.g. `CredentialResolver.describe()`,
`<redacted>` placeholder, an MCP tool that returns metadata
instead of secret material).

## When you must escalate

If a backend response accidentally surfaces a secret value (e.g.
an Auth0 SDK error envelope leaks the token), STOP, log a
generic error message, and surface the issue to the operator via
a structured audit event with the secret redacted. Do not paste
the leaked value into the transcript even to ask whether it
should have been redacted.
